package com.acme.billing;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;

@RestControllerAdvice
public class InvoiceExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(InvoiceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleNotFound(InvoiceNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                .body(ApiResponse.error("INVOICE_NOT_FOUND", ex.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(InvoiceNotCancellableException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleNotCancellable(InvoiceNotCancellableException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
                .body(ApiResponse.error("INVOICE_NOT_CANCELLABLE", ex.getMessage()));
    }
}
```

Convention decisions applied:
- Both rules (ownership, unpaid only) are enforced in the service, which uses constructor injection and no Lombok. Failures are domain exceptions that a `@RestControllerAdvice` maps to `ApiResponse.error(...)`, with no try/catch in a controller.
- Another customer's invoice returns the same 404 as a missing one, so invoice ids can't be probed. A paid or already-cancelled invoice returns 409.

I didn't write a controller, because I don't know how your security layer exposes the current customer id. It should be a thin `@RestController` that calls `cancel(id, customerId)` and returns `ApiResponse.ok(result)`. `@Transactional` assumes JPA dirty checking persists `markCancelled()`. If it doesn't, add a `save` to the repository.

